  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 316 AM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The broad surface high pressure centered over \nsouthern Ontario today will gradually shift eastward through the\nend of the week. An anomalously dry air mass and subsidence \nunder the ridging will keep skies mainly clear despite a weak \nfront sinking southward this morning. By this afternoon, \nnortherly flow will increase slightly, and temperatures will top\nout in the upper 50s to low 60s. Tonight will be an ideal \nradiational cooling night, with the dry air mass remaining \noverhead and winds becoming near calm toward sunrise. Lows will \nrange from the low to mid 20s in sheltered mountain valleys to \nthe low 30s elsewhere. \n\nThe quiet stretch of weather will continue Wednesday through \nThursday, with highs again forecast in the 50s to low 60s and lows \nin the 20s to 30s.   A weak boundary will move through Thursday, but \ngiven the dry air in place, the impacts of the frontal passage will \nbe minimal.  A few sprinkles may materialize, followed by increasing \nnortherly flow behind the front that will bring in a cooler air mass \nto close out the work week.  Breezy north winds can be expected \nThursday through Friday morning, especially channeled down the \nChamplain Valley and over mountain tops. High temperatures \nFriday will be a few degrees cooler than the previous days, \nranging from the mid 40s to low 50s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A 1034mb high pres wl be building into our region from \ncentral Canada late Friday into the upcoming weekend. This wl \nproduce a dry and cool northerly flow behind our weak cold frnt for \nFriday night into Saturday. Progged 850mb temps drop btwn -1C and -\n3C but start to modify quickly by Saturday. The building mid/upper \nlvl ridge and associated subsidence wl result in plenty of sunshine \nthis weekend with near seasonable temps. Expect a large diurnal \nswing from cool night lows in the mid 20s to mid 30s to daily highs \nin the mid 50s to lower 60s. Progged PW values are btwn 0.15 and \n0.25\", indicating how dry the atmospheric column wl be this upcoming \nweekend. The mid/upper lvl ridge slowly breaks down by next Tuesday \nwith approaching weak sfc boundary and a slight increase in moisture \nprofiles. Did note the latest WPC grids has 10-20% pops for this \ntime frame. In addition, some increase southerly flow ahead of \napproaching boundary is possible, which could increase fire weather \nconcerns early next week, given recent dry spell.",
